messages.reasoning_content:
Round-trip representations are stable, but synthetic flag is lost on round trip (because representation renders synthetic params as named assignations). So round-trip test should only assert representation equality, not synthetic flag. Good to know.

Now write the test file. Use JUnit 5 with @ParameterizedTest? That would need junit-jupiter-params (available). But keep it plain @Test with loops? Better clarity: use @Nested classes and parameterized tests with @MethodSource/@ValueSource maybe. Simpler: individual @Test methods with grouped assertions. I'll use @ParameterizedTest with @ValueSource for null/blank cases (needs junit-jupiter-params) — junit-jupiter aggregate artifact includes params. I'll depend on `org.junit.jupiter:junit-jupiter` (test scope) which includes api, params, engine.

Now let's write the test file carefully with expected values from probe.

Helper methods in test:
- `private static FragmentExpression parse(String input)`
- `private static String rep(IStandardExpression e)` returns e.getStringRepresentation()
- `private static List<String> assignations(FragmentExpression fe)` returns list of "left=right" strings.

Tests:

```java
package org.thymeleaf.standard.expression;

import static org.junit.jupiter.api.Assertions.*;

import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.Arrays;
import java.util.List;

import org.junit.jupiter.api.DisplayName;
import org.junit.jupiter.api.Nested;
import org.junit.jupiter.api.Test;
import org.junit.jupiter.params.ParameterizedTest;
import org.junit.jupiter.params.provider.NullSource;
import org.junit.jupiter.params.provider.ValueSource;
```

Test list:

A. Empty input family:
```java
@ParameterizedTest
@NullSource
@ValueSource(strings = {"", " ", "   ", "\t", "\n", " \t\r\n "})
void blankInputsReturnTheSharedEmptyFragmentExpression(String input) {
    assertSame(FragmentExpression.EMPTY_FRAGMENT_EXPRESSION, FragmentExpression.parseFragmentExpressionContent(input));
}
```

B. Template-only (no operator, no parens):
```java
@Test
void templateNameOnlyIsParsedAsATokenExpression() {
    FragmentExpression fe = guard("template");
    assertEquals("~{template}", fe.getStringRepresentation());
    assertEquals("template", rep(fe.getTemplateName()));
    assertInstanceOf(GenericTokenExpression.class, fe.getTemplateName());
    assertNull(fe.getFragmentSelector());
    assertFalse(fe.hasFragmentSelector());
    assertNull(fe.getParameters());
    assertFalse(fe.hasParameters());
    assertFalse(fe.hasSyntheticParameters());
}
```
Where `guard` asserts non-null and returns.

C. Surrounding whitespace trimmed: "  template  " and "template\t" -> same as above (rep "~{template}", template name "template").

D. template :: fragment: with variants "template :: frag", "template::frag", "  template  ::  frag  ", "template\t::\tfrag" → rep "~{template :: frag}", template token "template", selector token "frag", hasFragmentSelector true, no params, rep of getFragmentSelector "frag". Also assert `assertInstanceOf(GenericTokenExpression.class, fe.getFragmentSelector())`.

E. empty template name allowed: ":: frag", "::frag", " :: frag " → templateName null; selector token "frag"; hasFragmentSelector true; rep "~{ :: frag}".

F. Missing/blank fragment spec → null: "::", "::  ", "template ::", "template :: ", "t::", "  ::  ", "::\t", "t ::   ".

G. Quoted literals: "'t' :: 'f'" → both TextLiteralExpression, reps "'t'" and "'f'", rep "~{'t' :: 'f'}".

H. Variable expressions: "${var}" → templateName VariableExpression rep "${var}", selector null, rep "~{${var}}"; "template :: ${var}" → template token "template", selector VariableExpression "${var}".

Also "template :: frag (a=${x})" param right rep "${x}".

I. Assignation parameters: "template :: frag (a=1)" -> hasParameters, synthetic false, size 1, assignation "a=1", rep "~{template :: frag (a=1)}".
   Multi: "t :: f (a=1, b=${y}, c='z')" -> size 3, list equal to ["a=1", "b=${y}", "c='z'"], synthetic false, rep "~{t :: f (a=1,b=${y},c='z')}".
   Duplicate names: "t :: f (a=1, a=2)" → size 2.
   Whitespace-heavy: " t  ::  f  (  a  =  1  ) " → rep "~{t :: f (a=1)}", params single "a=1".
   Trailing/leading commas: "t :: f (a=1,)" → size 1; "t :: f (,a=1)" → size 1.

J. Synthetic parameters: "template :: frag (1,2)" → synthetic true, size 2, names '_arg0','_arg1', values "1","2"; rep "~{template :: frag ('_arg0'=1,'_arg1'=2)}".
   "t :: f (x)" → synthetic true size 1 name '_arg0' value 'x'.
   "t :: f (1+2)" → synthetic true, value rep "(1 + 2)".
   "t :: f (|text|)" → synthetic true, value "'text'".
   "t :: f (('nested'))" → synthetic true, value "'nested'".
   "t :: f('a)b')" → synthetic true, single param value "'a)b'".
   "t :: f ('a, b')" → synthetic true size 1.

K. Parameters with empty fragment spec when no operator: "template (a=1)" → fragmentSelector null, hasFragmentSelector false, params ["a=1"], synthetic false, rep "~{template (a=1)}"; "template (1,2)" → synthetic true, selector null, rep "~{template ('_arg0'=1,'_arg1'=2)}".

L. Parentheses not treated as parameters:
   - "template :: frag ()" → no parameters, fragment selector TextLiteralExpression "frag ()", rep "~{template :: 'frag ()'}".
   - "t :: f ()" same.
   - "(1,2)" → template name text literal rep "'(1,2)'", no params, selector null.
   - "(a=1)" → template text literal "'(a=1)'".

M. Unbalanced parens: "template :: frag (" → fragment selector text literal "frag (" no params. Also "t :: f (a=1) extra" → selector text literal "f (a=1) extra", no params. And "tmpl :: frag (a=1) :: other" → template token "tmpl", selector text literal "frag (a=1) :: other", no params.

N. Invalid parameter lists → null: "t :: f (a=)", "t :: f (=1)", "t :: f (a=1, 2)", "t :: f(())", "template :: frag(text())".
   Careful with "(a=1, 2)" which was null. And "t :: f ( )" null.

O. Escaped quotes: "t :: f (a='it\\'s')" → single param "a='it\\'s'". Also "t :: f(a='(')" → param "a='('" (parens inside quotes ignored).

P. Whole-input literal: "a'b" → template name TextLiteralExpression "a'b" printed as "'a\\'b'" ; representation "~{'a\\'b'}". In Java string: assertEquals("~{'a\\'b'}", ...). Hmm careful: The rep printed was `~{'a\'b'}` meaning characters: ~ { ' a \ ' b ' }. In Java: "~{'a\\'b'}".

Q. "~{template :: frag}" is treated as raw content (not unwrapped): template name text literal "~{template", selector text literal "frag}". Values printed: `~{'~{template' :: 'frag}'}`. In Java: "~{'~{template' :: 'frag'}".

Hmm, is that a meaningful test? It documents that parseFragmentExpressionContent does not strip the ~{...} wrapper (that's parseFragmentExpression's job). Reasonable but a bit odd. I could include it as documenting behavior. I'll include in round-trip test set instead maybe. I'll include explicit test with display name "tildeWrapperIsNotRemovedByContentParsing".

R. Determinism/idempotency: parse same input twice → equal representations, and both synthetic same.

S. Round-trip: for a set of inputs where result non-null, parseFragmentExpression(rep) yields same representation. Only assert representation (synthetic not preserved).

Also possible: nested fragment expressions inside params: "t :: f (a=~{x :: y})" - check probe? Not tested. Let's check quickly along with a couple extras: "t :: f (a=~{x :: y})", "t :: f ('a,b' , 3)".

Let me probe those before writing final.

